; 48-profile.kurt

; `kurt --profile FILE` checks the file as usual and then prints, on stderr, its ten slowest lines with the time and the number
; of search steps of each -- where a proof is slow:
;
;     the slowest lines of proof.kurt (1.37 s in all, seconds and search steps):
;         0.85 s        0 steps      1  load logic, number
;         0.40 s     1200 steps     14  x + y = y + x
;
; (a `load` line has the time of the files it loads; a file checked before costs nothing). A line that takes seconds is
; usually a goal that doesn't follow in one step, so the search tries every rule and fact: split it into steps, or give
; the fact it needs. From Python, `kurt.RunConfig(profile=True)` fills `CheckResult.profile` (and `--json`): a record
; `file`, `line`, `seconds`, `steps` for each line.
;
; In an editor with the language server (VS Code: the extension), the same shows while you write: the line
; being checked is tinted while a check runs, and afterwards every line gets an orange to red background, the slower the
; redder (`kurt.heat.enabled`); a line of 2 s or more also has an Information diagnostic with its time. The status item
; says `Kurt: checking 12/84` and its menu can cancel the check.
;
; The file below is checked as every lesson is; run `kurt --profile keywords/48-profile.kurt` to see its own profile.

load logic
prop A, B
use A implies B
use A
B                       ; one step: "impl-elim" of the two facts
